<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Canterbury</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Durovernum Cantiacorum</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Canterbury is a cathedral city in Kent, south-east <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. As Durovernum Cantiacorum it was the chief town of the Cantiaci under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>, and it became the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anglo-saxons">Anglo-Saxon</a> kingdom of Kent, where Augustine founded in 597 the see whose archbishop heads the English Church. The murder of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thomas-becket">Thomas Becket</a> in its cathedral in 1170 made it a great place of pilgrimage.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/canterbury.jpg" alt="Canterbury" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antony McCallum: Who is the uploader, photographer, full copyright owner and proprietor of WyrdLight.com · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canterbury-cathedral-wyrdlight.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Canterbury lies on the River Stour, not far from its mouth on the Strait of Dover, in east Kent. The site has been occupied since prehistoric times. The Roman name Durovernum Cantiacorum means Durovernum of the Cantiaci, and replaced an earlier British name reconstructed as Durouernon, meaning stronghold by the alder grove. In Sub-Roman Britain it was Cair Ceint, the stronghold of Kent, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jutes">Jutes</a> who occupied it called it Cantwareburh in Old English.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Canterbury was first recorded as the main settlement of the Cantiaci. In the 1st century CE the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Romans</a> captured it and rebuilt it with a grid of streets, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a>, a temple, a forum and public baths. They kept no major garrison, but its position on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/watling-street">Watling Street</a>, in relation to the ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/richborough">Richborough</a>, Dover and Lymne, gave it strategic importance. In the late 3rd century they built an earth bank and a wall with seven gates round it. It later served as capital of the Jute kingdom of Kent.</p><p>A large <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> army besieged Canterbury in 1011, and the city was pillaged. Remembering the destruction caused by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danes">Danes</a>, its inhabitants did not resist <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-the-conqueror">William the Conqueror</a> in 1066, and William ordered a wooden castle built beside the Roman wall, rebuilt in stone in the early 12th century. In the mid-16th century Canterbury hosted the first congregation of Huguenot refugees in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, founded around 1548 and scattered in 1553–4 under Mary I. In 1647, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/english-civil-war">English Civil War</a>, the Plum Pudding Riots broke out, and the trial of the rioters led to a Kentish revolt.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Canterbury is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a> and its cathedral is the seat of the Archbishop of Canterbury. Its surviving past includes a city wall founded in Roman times and rebuilt in the 14th century, the Westgate Towers, the ruins of St Augustine's Abbey and the Norman castle. The Canterbury and Whitstable Railway, which ran from 1830 to 1953, was the first regular passenger steam railway in the world. The city's economy rests on tourism, higher education and retail.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Canterbury” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Church, under the archbishops of <b>Canterbury</b> and York, owned a large share of the land until Henry VIII dissolved the monasteries between 1536 and 1541.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-england">Kingdom of England · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…quality, including the carvings at Breedon on the Hill and the church at Brixworth, and Mercian scholars mattered to the later English revival: Alfred the Great recruited four Mercian clerics, among them Wærferth of Worcester and Plegmund, later archbishop of <b>Canterbury</b>, to help with his programme of learning.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-mercia">Kingdom of Mercia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Most scholars place its making in or around <b>Canterbury</b>.</blockquote><a href="/work/bayeux-tapestry">Bayeux Tapestry · How It Was Made</a></li><li><blockquote>Stephen Langton, Archbishop of <b>Canterbury</b>, who had worked with the barons on their demands, was asked to organise peace talks.</blockquote><a href="/work/magna-carta">Magna Carta · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>The Tales are presented as part of a fictional storytelling contest held by a group of pilgrims travelling from London to <b>Canterbury</b> to visit the shrine of Saint Thomas Becket at Canterbury Cathedral.</blockquote><a href="/work/the-canterbury-tales">The Canterbury Tales · What It Is</a></li><li><blockquote>Learning and royal service could carry a man of modest birth to these offices: Thomas Becket, a London merchant’s son, became Archbishop of <b>Canterbury</b> in 1162.</blockquote><a href="/societies/feudal-europe">Social Hierarchy of Feudal Europe</a></li></ul></section>
